   The ratio-in-r column converges to 9/8 = 1.125:  F_r(2,b)/F_r(1,b) = Theta((9/8)^r).
   Reason: F_r(2,0) = (phi_r/4^r) sum_{i<=r} 2^{r-i} C(2r+1,i), whose summand
   peaks at i=(2r-1)/3 INSIDE the range, so the sum is ~ (3/2)^{2r+1} 2^r = 
   1.5*(9/2)^r, giving F_r(2,0) ~ 1.5 phi_r (9/8)^r.

==================================================================================================
G1.  The factorial amplifier, and the one-line fix.
==================================================================================================
   ORIGINAL (ATTEMPT.md SS5/SS6):
     D_r(b) = [ r C_{r-1}(b) + A_r(b) ] / (r+b+1)
     C_r(b) = B_r(b) + r C_{r-1}(b+1) + 2 D_r(b+1)
   The C-step multiplies by r with NO compensating division -> C_r >~ r! .
   The discarded factor is legitimate: the term being bounded is
     (r/n) eps^h_{r-1}(a,b+1,n),  |eps^h_{r-1}| <= C_{r-1}(b+1)/n^2,
   and the standing hypothesis is n >= b+r+1, so r/n <= r/(b+r+1) < 1.
   Also (1-s)-(1+b+r)/n = (n-1-a-b-r)/n lies in [0,1] on the valid domain,
   so the factor 2 in front of D_r(b+1) can be 1.
   IMPROVED:
     D'_r(b) = [ r C'_{r-1}(b) + A_r(b) ] / (r+b+1)
     C'_r(b) = B_r(b) + [r/(b+r+1)] C'_{r-1}(b+1) + D'_r(b+1)

